9. Orders by customers who placed more than one order

This one is a little more interesting because the subquery returns multiple customer names.

So we use IN:

SELECT *
FROM sales_orders
WHERE customer_name IN (
    SELECT customer_name
    FROM sales_orders
    GROUP BY customer_name
    HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
);
Important

Don't use:

=

because the subquery can return several customers.

Use:

IN
10. Price greater than every Furniture price
SELECT *
FROM sales_orders
WHERE price > ALL (
    SELECT price
    FROM sales_orders
    WHERE category = 'Furniture'
);
ALL means:

The price must be greater than every single value returned by the subquery.

For example, if Furniture prices were:

4500
8500
12000
30000

the order must be greater than 30,000.

11. Price greater than at least one Furniture price
SELECT *
FROM sales_orders
WHERE price > ANY (
    SELECT price
    FROM sales_orders
    WHERE category = 'Furniture'
);
Difference between Q10 and Q11
Operator	Meaning
> ALL	Greater than every value
> ANY	Greater than at least one value

This distinction is very important for subqueries.

12. Customers with order price greater than average order price
SELECT DISTINCT customer_name
FROM sales_orders
WHERE price > (
    SELECT AVG(price)
    FROM sales_orders
);
Why DISTINCT?

A customer could have multiple orders satisfying the condition.

DISTINCT ensures that the customer appears only once.

